Installation instructions

To install Pascal-P6, execute:

[Linux]

> ./configure
> . ./setpath
> make

[Windows]

> configure
> setpath
> make

setpath puts the project's bin directory on the path and sets PASCALP6, the
project root the makefile builds its paths from. It has to be sourced (the
leading ". " on Linux) rather than run, because a program cannot set the
environment of the shell that called it, and it has to come before make.

Note that you can copy the path set within the setpath or setpath.bat batch
file to your startup environment to prevent the need to execute this again.

configure checks the external tools and libraries the build needs and, on a
Debian or Ubuntu host, installs the missing ones with apt-get. These are the
libraries the Ami models bind to -- OpenSSL for network, ALSA and fluidsynth
for sound, X11 with XTest for graphics, FreeType and FontConfig for its
fonts -- along with the static closure of those, because Pascaline links
fully static.

The static ALSA and fluidsynth archives are not carried by any distribution,
so configure builds them from source with tools/staticdeps/build.sh when they
are absent. That takes some minutes and fetches both sources from the
network.

To see what is missing without installing or building anything, run:

> ./configure --no-install

The packages it names are Debian and Ubuntu names. On another distribution,
install the equivalents by hand and run configure again.

You can then compile and run single Pascal sources with the command:

P6 <file>

The extension .pas is supplied automatically.
